# ibus -- the input method framework, packaged because gnome-shell will not
# start without its typelib.
#
# BLFS 12.4 is the book this desktop tier follows and it pins 1.5.32. The
# VERSION here is the book's; the ARTEFACT is not, and that difference is the
# most important line in this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RELEASE TARBALL, NOT THE ONE THE BOOK LINKS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BLFS downloads https://github.com/ibus/ibus/archive/1.5.32/ibus-1.5.32.tar.gz
# -- GitHub's auto-generated snapshot of the git tree, 1.5 MB. It contains no
# `configure`, which is why the book's very next command is `./autogen.sh`.
# That one line drags in FOUR packages this tree does not have: autoconf,
# automake, intltool and Vala. Vala is not optional there either: ibus's entire
# gtk3 panel UI is written in Vala, 29 .vala files, and the snapshot ships the
# sources without the generated C.
#
# The RELEASE asset attached to the same tag -- 4.2 MB, same version -- is a
# proper `make dist` tarball. Verified by listing it rather than by assuming:
# it carries `configure`, `Makefile.in`, `aclocal.m4`, and 21 generated .c files
# in ui/gtk3 beside the .vala originals they came from. So it builds with
# nothing but ./configure and make, and the four-package bootstrap disappears.
#
# THE VERSION PIN IS UNCHANGED AND THAT IS THE POINT. The book's authority is
# over WHICH RELEASE this tree ships, and 1.5.32 is what it says. Which of a
# release's own artefacts to build from is a packaging decision, and choosing
# the one upstream produced with `make dist` is the conservative half of it --
# the snapshot is the derived thing here, not the tarball.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/ibus/ibus/releases/download/1.5.32/ibus-1.5.32.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=b24f41ae38b236b254c09f1a8f53c2354b69b0789e89cea888d0494b09d15d67
SRC_DIR=ibus-1.5.32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --enable-introspection=yes, AND NOT THE DEFAULT `auto`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is the whole reason the package is in the tree, so it is the one thing
# that must not be allowed to degrade quietly. `auto` searches, and if
# gobject-introspection is missing or unusable it builds a perfectly good ibus
# WITHOUT the typelib -- which installs, passes every link check, satisfies
# pkg-config, and leaves gnome-shell failing with the exact error this package
# was added to fix. `yes` turns that into a configure failure.
#
# The install assertion checks the same thing from the other end, on the staged
# tree, because a flag that was accepted is not a file that was produced.
#
# --enable-vala=no. Vala is not packaged, the C is pre-generated, and what
# --enable-vala builds is the .vapi BINDINGS for people writing Vala against
# ibus -- not anything gnome-shell touches, which reaches ibus through the GIR.
# Left at its default `auto` this is a search that currently finds nothing;
# said explicitly, it is a decision. (Finding: a boolean `auto` is a probe, not
# a switch -- the day valac appears in the tree, `auto` would silently start
# building bindings nobody asked for.)
#
# --disable-xim. The XIM server is ON BY DEFAULT (the option is spelled
# "--disable-xim: Do not build xim server") and it is an X11 input method
# server, which needs an X server to serve. This tree runs none: mutter is
# built -Dx11=false, and the only X here is Xwayland, rooted under the Wayland
# session. Wayland input methods go through the wayland support below.
#
# --enable-wayland. Auto-enabled when libxkbcommon and wayland are both
# present, which they are -- and written out anyway, because "it happened to be
# detected" and "this tree requires it" are different statements and only one
# of them survives a dependency being reordered.
#
# --enable-gtk4 and the gtk3 module left on: both toolkits are in this desktop,
# and an im module for only one of them is a desktop where half the
# applications cannot use an input method. --disable-gtk2: there is no gtk2.
#
# --disable-emoji-dict and --disable-unicode-dict. BOTH are disabled for one
# reason, and it is mechanical rather than editorial: they are built from the
# Unicode Character Database, which BLFS downloads SEPARATELY as UCD.zip and
# unpacks before configuring. This tree's fetch.sh takes exactly one SRC_URL,
# and there is no unzip in the package set either. Disabling them is what makes
# ibus a single-source package. What is lost is ibus's own emoji picker
# dictionary and its unicode-name search -- neither is on the path gnome-shell
# uses, which is the IBus panel/engine protocol. If someone later wants the
# emoji picker, the work is a second source, not a flag.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --disable-ui, AND IT IS THE ONE FLAG HERE THAT A BUILD FOUND RATHER THAN A
# READING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ibus's default user interface is a GTK3 panel with its own candidate window.
# GNOME does not use it: gnome-shell IS the IBus panel -- js/misc/ibusManager.js
# registers the shell as the panel service and js/ui/ibusCandidatePopup.js draws
# the candidates. Building it here would ship a second, competing candidate
# window for a desktop that already has one.
#
# It is also the only part of ibus that fails to build in this tree, and the
# failure is an upstream under-linking bug that only strict linkers see:
#
#   keybindingmanager.o: undefined reference to symbol 'XIUngrabKeycode'
#   /usr/lib/libXi.so.6: error adding symbols: DSO missing from command line
#
# ui/gtk3/keybindingmanager.c grabs global hotkeys through XInput2, and
# ui/gtk3/Makefile.am adds only @X11_LIBS@ to its link line -- XIGrabKeycode and
# XIUngrabKeycode are in libXi, which is never named. On a toolchain that
# resolves indirect DSOs the link succeeds by accident; on a modern ld it does
# not.
#
# So there were two candidate fixes and they are not equivalent: patch the link
# line and ship a panel this desktop must not run, or drop the component. The
# component goes. Note this ALSO removes the last X11 linkage from the package,
# on a tree whose mutter is built -Dx11=false -- an X-only global-hotkey grabber
# had no way to work here regardless of whether it linked.
#
# --disable-ui does NOT touch the Wayland support: ENABLE_UI gates ui/, while
# ENABLE_WAYLAND gates client/wayland/, a different directory. Checked in the
# Makefile.am files rather than assumed from the option names, because the
# generated ibus-ui-wayland.desktop lives under ui/gtk3 and makes the two look
# like one thing.
#
# --disable-tests, AND ITS HELP STRING IS WRONG ABOUT WHAT IT DOES. The option
# reads "--disable-tests: Do not run tests", which sounds like it leaves them
# built and merely unexecuted -- i.e. useless to us. The Makefile.am says
# otherwise: bus/Makefile.am has `noinst_PROGRAMS = $(TESTS)` and defines TESTS
# only inside `if ENABLE_TESTS`, so disabling it empties noinst_PROGRAMS and the
# test programs are never COMPILED. That is what this tree needs, because
# bus/test-client.c is an X11 program -- XOpenDisplay, XKeysymToKeycode,
# XConvertCase -- and its link line names no X libraries, so `make all` fails on
# a strict linker before it ever gets to `make check`:
#
#   test_stress-test-client.o: undefined reference to `XOpenDisplay'
#
# Found by building. Read the Makefile.am to learn what an option does, not the
# help string -- prose describes intent and the build files describe behaviour,
# and here they disagree. (Sibling of the --nodaemon lesson: check an option
# against the thing that implements it, never against its documentation.)
#
# --disable-appindicator: libdbusmenu is not packaged. --disable-python2: there
# is no python2, and it also switches off the python support library, which
# needs dbus-python -- also not packaged. --disable-systemd-services: units for
# a systemd user session that does not exist here, the same call gnome-shell,
# at-spi2-core and NetworkManager all record.
#
# --sysconfdir=/etc because ibus installs an xinput config and a dconf profile
# and the default would put them under /usr/etc.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--sysconfdir=/etc \
	--enable-introspection=yes \
	--enable-vala=no \
	--enable-wayland \
	--enable-gtk4 \
	--disable-gtk2 \
	--disable-xim \
	--disable-ui \
	--disable-tests \
	--disable-emoji-dict \
	--disable-unicode-dict \
	--disable-appindicator \
	--disable-python2 \
	--disable-systemd-services"
